<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siete Partidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1256–<a href="/years/1265">1265</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siete Partidas, or Seven-Part Code, is a law code compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under Alfonso X, drawing on Roman and canon law. It covered government, the church, family, property and crime, but gained force of law only through the Ordinance of Alcalá of 1348. It remained a foundation of law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its American empire, and was cited in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> into the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siete-partidas.jpg" alt="Siete Partidas" width="189" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partidas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The code was first called the Libro de las Leyes, the Book of Laws, and took its present name, from the number of its sections, only in the 14th century. One of its oldest versions says a commission of the leading Castilian jurists wrote it under Alfonso X's personal direction between 26 June 1256 and 28 August 1265; other spans have been proposed, but most historians hold that it was not completed until 1265. Its aim is debated: Alfonso was pursuing the crown of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and Aquilino Iglesias argued that the code was meant for the whole Empire, while García-Gallo and others pointed to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-language">Spanish language</a> and its references to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>'s own territorial organisation.</p><p>Resistance, above all among the nobility, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> to pass the Ordinances of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> in 1274, which restricted the code to cases under the king's own jurisdiction and left other matters to local fueros. Whether Alfonso X enacted it is unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A prologue sets out the work's purpose, and each of the seven partidas begins with a letter of the king's name, forming the acrostic Alfonso. The parts contain 182 articles made up of 2802 laws, covering constitutional, civil, commercial and criminal law and procedure. Its provisions come with references to authors and texts, examples and a reasoned account of their religious, philosophical and historical background, and it has been called a humanist encyclopedia. Because each partida was written by a different person, some provisions contradict others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The main editions were those of Alonso Díaz de Montalvo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, 1491), of Gregorio López (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, 1555), authorised by royal decree on 7 September 1555 and most used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, and of the Real Academia de la Historia (1807). The code was studied in universities and translated into Catalan, Portuguese, Galician and English. It reached Spanish America with Castilian law and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> with Portuguese law, stayed in force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> until the codifications of 1822–1916, and gave the legal basis for the juntas formed after the imprisonment of Fernando VII in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>. Samuel Parsons Scott's English translation appeared in 1931.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siete Partidas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alfonso X, known as the Wise, issued the Fuero Real and the great legal code of the <b>Siete Partidas</b>, which gained force of law only in 1348 under Alfonso XI but became a foundation of Castilian and later Spanish American law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish law, from the <b>Siete Partidas</b> on, left them some rights: to marry in church, to keep savings and to buy their freedom, often in instalments (coartación), and freedom was commoner in towns than in the countryside.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
